From 7976b8f02a1ea566bcf0ced496cec56e327f0d03 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dr Jonathan Richard Robert Kimmitt <jonathan@kimmitt.uk>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 11:03:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] map: a port without a pin, or a pin assignment to no
 port, is fatal

The electrical rule check gains the netlist's ports.  With a constraints
file that assigns package pins, every port bit must have one, and every
set_property LOC / PACKAGE_PIN ... [get_ports ...] must name a port bit of
the netlist as the netlist names it.

A port declared as a one-bit vector, output [0:0] cs_n, is the scalar cs_n
in the netlist.  legend64's constraints said cs_n[0], for the clock pair,
clock enable, chip select and termination of its DDR3: the five lines
assigned nothing, nextpnr placed the pads where it liked without a word,
and LVS listed the ports as skipped.  Vivado, given the same netlist and
file, refused to write a bitstream.

The pins come from the file --map-only-sdc reads, which already skipped
these lines; --map-only-pins <xdc> reads the pin assignments alone, for a
file whose clocks the timing reader does not take (clocks on nets).  A
file with no pin assignments checks nothing; one that names ports by a
pattern has its names checked, not its coverage.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
